Subject: [PATCH 02568/11362] [outer-loop] click-capable exploration: built +
 correct; ka59 blocker is object-RELEVANCE, not clicks

Added click-capable exploration for the push/click class: clicks on PIECE-object centroids (bounded
~#pieces, not every pixel), enabled on the escalated pieces-key pass + the solve. ka59's win setup
needs a click (C:1), so this was the named next gap.

RESULT (results/arc3_test_time_goal_induction.json, fully first-contact, no banked solve):
  sp80/cn04/ar25: still solve on the AGENT pass (4/14/15 actions) -- NO regression (clicks only
    activate on the escalated pass).
  ka59: still NOT stumbled, even with click-capable + multi-object escalation at 15k expansions.

ROOT CAUSE (debugged, not guessed): ka59's pieces-key tracks SEVEN objects and ALL 9 candidates
change the state, so the search space is ~positions^7 x click-branching -- an 11-action win is
intractable for brute BFS at any reasonable budget. The clicks and multi-object state are CORRECT;
the combinatorics defeat brute search. Tracking 7 objects is OVER-fragmentation (only 1-2 are
relevant to the win).

THE REAL BINDING CONSTRAINT (sharpened): object RELEVANCE, not search mechanics. Track just the agent
-> too few (misses the block); track all 7 pieces -> too many (intractable). The unlock is RELEVANCE
FILTERING: track only the agent + the 1-2 objects the win actually depends on -- which requires
understanding the win condition at the object level (goal-induction depth), NOT more brute force.

NET: all the MECHANICS are now in place (clicks, multi-object state, adaptive escalation), 3/4 games
solve first-contact with no regression, and ka59's residual gap is precisely characterized as
object-relevance filtering -- a goal-induction problem, not an exploration-budget problem.
Adversarial-verify: clean.

